@ndivisibleyork', 'datetime': '2026-10-17T00:00:00.000Z', 'id': '4010050037215551072', 'image_url': 'https://scontent-dfw5-1.cdninstagram.com/v/t51.82787-15/851828864_17906594124576349_8848954497788644750_n.webp?stp=dst-jpg_e35_s640x640_tt6&_nc_cat=106&ccb=7-5&_nc_sid=18de74&efg=eyJlZmdfdGFnIjoiRkVFRC5iZXN0X2ltYWdlX3VybGdlbi5DMyJ9&_nc_ohc=sEFH0OrLirkQ7kNvwEmLyvt&_nc_oc=AdoSNfd1htXyOiDGVShG4C302t9e5_M6LqcUbS2-TKz0icp-t8YW_aOyJb_LP-dd7fw&_nc_zt=23&_nc_ht=scontent-dfw5-1.cdninstagram.com&_nc_gid=tx0EctHD1sMnH6uYeQXLAA&_nc_ss=7a689&oh=00_AQRmqwAzTKoRuI_RNqQcpZl3n2JMZIn4sqgPxSsMzz0Jiw&oe=6AED5FF4', 'post_hashtags': None, 'content_type': 'Image', 'url': 'https://www.instagram.com/p/Demj0M_xl5g'}, {'caption': 'Don't look away.

"In this city that was once a critical stop on the Underground Railroad for Black people fleeing slavery, it feels like a bleak period in our history is repeating. 
Some 161 years after the end of the Civil War, Black and brown immigrants who live in this blue-collar city of rowhouse neighborhoods, or scattered among York County’s 450,000 residents, are once again terrified of leaving their homes, knowing they are one police siren away from losing their freedom.
...As many as 12,000 Haitians who left their violent homeland to settle in York County with protected legal immigration status have now become, in essence, sitting ducks for local cops who’ve signed cooperation pacts with U.S. Customs and Immigration Enforcement (ICE). The Donald Trump regime yanked that protection, known as TPS, in late July.
...There’s a powerful case to be made that the surge in arrests by local police, and the fear that is keeping families behind locked doors and drawn shades, has made York County the most dangerous place for immigrants in the entire northeastern United States.
...Michelle Hines, a community activist with Solidarity Lancaster, who’s called on her scientific background to analyze 287(g) arrests in south-central Pennsylvania, told me that many immigrants in Lancaster County must pass through Hellam on their way to immigration hearings or other government services in York. She begs them to somehow find a different route.
'It’s better to not have a job than to drive through Hellam,' she said. Hines said her analysis of an early batch of DHS records showed that 80% of the 287(g) arrestees in York County did not have a criminal record or pending charges." -- Will Bunch, Philly Inquirer columnist 

Also from this column: The best statewide numbers ... show that more than 31% of all 287(g) arrests made in Pennsylvania from the start of 2025 through early August took place in York County, even though it only comprises 3.6% of the state’s population.

Here's a link https://share.inquirer.com/03Exny

